{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Api-Cloudtrail-2013-11-01","context":"#### Result Details\n\n##### Members\n\n**AdvancedEventSelectors**\n: : **Type:** *Array of AdvancedEventSelector structures*\n\n The advanced event selectors that are configured for the trail.\n\n**EventSelectors**\n: : **Type:** *Array of EventSelector structures*\n\n The event selectors that are configured for the trail.\n\n**TrailARN**\n: : **Type:** *string*\n\n The specified trail ARN that has the event selectors","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-sdk-php/v3/api/api-cloudtrail-2013-11-01.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"getEventSelectors","context":"cloudtrail/aws.sdk.kotlin.services.cloudtrail/getEventSelectors","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdk-for-kotlin/api/latest/cloudtrail/aws.sdk.kotlin.services.cloudtrail/get-event-selectors.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Enable cross-account queries on AWS CloudTrail lake using delegated administration from AWS Organizations | AWS Cloud Operations Blog","context":"## STEP 4: Connect to member account to query event data store\n\n1. Login to the member account with the shared IAM role console login link. Now this member account can query the event data store without needing to go through the management account.\n\n2. The role has been switched as seen in below screenshot\n\nFigure 14: The member account has assumed IAM role which was attached to it\n\n3. Now navigate to the CloudTrail Lake and you will be able to see the event data store created by using a delegated admin account. This allows the member account to query the event data store without the need to login to the management account.\n\nFigure 15: Access CloudTrail Lake event data store\n\n4. Looking at the details of this event data store, you will see that the ARN refers to the delegated admin account and delete option is greyed out under action drop down. This shows that member accounts can only perform tasks limited to the permissions granted to their account\n\nFigure 16: Access event data store from member account\n\n5. Run queries on event data store from the member account. User action on event data store can be controlled by the IAM policy attached to the role.\n\nFigure 17: Run queries on event data store","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/enable-cross-account-queries-on-aws-cloudtrail-lake-using-delegated-administration-from-aws-organizations/"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS CloudTrail announces delegated administrator account support for AWS Organizations","context":"AWS CloudTrail has introduced support for delegated administrator accounts, enabling customers to manage organization trails and CloudTrail Lake event data stores from an account other than the management account in AWS Organizations. The management account can delegate CloudTrail administrative actions to a member account, such as a security and logging account, while retaining ownership of all CloudTrail organization resources","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2022/11/aws-cloudtrail-delegated-account-support-aws-organizations/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS Organizations emits CloudTrail events for account membership changes - AWS","context":"AWS Organizations now emits CloudTrail events to the management account whenever accounts join or leave the organization. These new events, AccountJoinedOrganization and AccountDepartedOrganization, provide enhanced visibility into organizational membership changes. The AccountJoinedOrganization event captures how an account joined (Created or Invited) and the join timestamp. The AccountDepartedOrganization event records how an account departed (Left for voluntary departures, Removed for account removal by the management account, or Cleaned for permanently closed accounts) and the departure timestamp. These events can be used to create CloudWatch alarms or Amazon EventBridge rules for real-time notifications, enabling rapid response to suspicious organizational changes. These CloudTrail events are available in all commercial, AWS GovCloud (US), and China Regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/05/aws-organizations-cloudtrail/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS Config now supports organization-wide resource data aggregation in a delegated administrator account","context":"AWS Config, a service that helps assess, audit, and evaluate AWS resource configurations, now supports organization-wide resource data aggregation in a delegated administrator account. Previously, aggregators could collect configuration and compliance data from multiple accounts and regions, or from an organization and its member accounts","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2020/11/aws-config-supports-organization-wide-resource-data-aggregation-delegated-administrator-account/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"AWS Backup now supports cross-account management and cross-account backup in the AWS European Sovereign Cloud - AWS","context":"AWS Backup now supports cross-account management, cross-account backup, and delegated administrators in the AWS European Sovereign Cloud (Germany) Region. With cross-account management, users can deploy organization-wide backup policies from their AWS Organizations management account or a delegated administrator account and monitor backup, copy, and restore jobs across all accounts from one place. Cross-account backup allows users to copy recovery points to a backup vault in another account, adding protection against accidental or malicious deletion in the source account. Delegated administrators can be registered to manage backups across the organization without needing access to the management account. These capabilities are free to use, with standard AWS Backup pricing applying. For more information, visit the AWS Backup documentation and console","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/aws-backup-cross-account-esc/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"amazon-eventbridge-event-bus","skill_description":"Builds, runs, debugs, and operates event-driven applications using EventBridge Event Bus - a managed, centrally governed publish/subscribe event bus that an organization can share across many teams and accounts. Applicable when workloads need event-driven architectures, decoupling, choreography, asynchronous integration, pub/sub, fan-out, event ordering, delivered in sequence, event router, event broker, event bus, event store, event retention, event replay, deduplication, avro, protobuf, cloudevents, uses events or messages. Designed as a centrally managed event bus platform allowing governance and control - such as subscriber control, revocation, per-account throttling - while giving application owners flexibility across an organization spanning multiple accounts, with end-to-end open-standards observability and cost allocation. Also helps reduce costs in high fan-out scenarios where multi-account forwarding adds cost. A serverless, managed alternative to self-managed event streaming platforms.\n\nServices: eventbridge, sqs, sns, lambda, kinesis, step-functions, dynamodb\nTasks: architect, configure, debug, govern, migrate, observe, optimize, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, architect, platform-engineer\nWorkload: event-driven, messaging, serverless\n*","skill_name":"amazon-eventbridge-event-bus"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify CloudTrail GetEventSelectors behavior for organization trails from member accounts"}
